[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-47922"] :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,{x}^{2}+1, & x<1 \\\\ -x+2, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[0,2]$\n- $(1,2)$\n- $[-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[0,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}({x}^{2}+1)=(1)^{2}+1=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(-x+2)=-1+2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(1,2)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[-2,2].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=1,$ which is belongs to the interval $[-2,2].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-2,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"None\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-73487/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "efe0b73c-d92b-5f79-847c-85c109010815" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73487/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "2b82ea67-b6cf-5760-bf11-14d7edd39c05" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73487/selection/df67