#uuid "400114d5-9626-599a-89e9-6bf1cca46396" :question/math-academy-id "q-129221" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)?$   \n\n- $[0,1]$\n- $[1,2]$\n- $(0,2)$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[0,1]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because it is not defined at $x=0.$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,2]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,2].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(0,2)$  is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-129221/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-129221"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-131180/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "4c8fdec3-ec81-5e6c-8470-37ed33c6fed5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131180/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "c57043e5-9a62-507d-bd57-bace8bd3ab2e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131180/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "36f3cc6a-82e9-5662-a467-0725cc5f3bc9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131180/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "e9a307de-2bf9-5ede-9b9d-2d27468bc0dc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131180/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "1af6c891-e9aa-5cf1-88c3-8839f390e38d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131180/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ef5f6a25-9b99-51e9-b5e2-f20595799711" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-131180/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-131180/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-131180/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-131180/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-131180/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-131180/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-131180" :question/id #uuid "39192d32-1d30-5a83-a103-b59ae37c1939" :question/math-academy-id "q-131180" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{3}, & -1\\le x<1 \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $(-1,1)$\n- $[-2,0]$\n- $[0,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $(-1,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[-2,0].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}(-1)=-1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}({x}^{3})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=-1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[-2,0],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[0,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}({x}^{3})=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(1)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-131180/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-131180"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
